Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = stylized children's picture-book illustration; painted storybook look, soft watercolor edges, warm nursery light, velveteen textures.

FADE IN:

EXT. HOUSE - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Snow dusts a quiet cottage roof. A nursery window glows warm gold.

(SOUND: distant sleigh bells, soft wind in bare branches)

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A painted storybook nursery. Stockings hang by the fender. At the top of the Boy's stocking sits the VELVETEEN RABBIT — fat and bunchy, spotted brown and white velveteen, real thread whiskers, ears lined with pink sateen, a sprig of holly between his paws.

(SOUND: tissue paper rustling, children's delighted gasps)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

Nuts, oranges, a toy engine, and a clockwork mouse tumble from the stocking. THE BOY — a small child with bright eyes and rumpled hair — hugs the Rabbit tight for a long, happy moment.

(SOUND: clockwork mouse whirring)

Aunts and Uncles fill the doorway with parcels. In the rush of new presents, the Rabbit is set aside on the nursery floor.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him, and then, in the excitement, the Velveteen Rabbit was forgotten.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Weeks later. The Rabbit sits alone near the toy cupboard among superior mechanical toys. A MODEL BOAT, paint worn, and TIMOTHY the jointed wooden lion look down their noses. Only the SKIN HORSE — ancient, brown coat bald in patches, seams showing, tail hairs sparse — lies kind beside him near the nursery fender.

(SOUND: nursery clock ticking, coals settling in the grate)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a long time he lived in the toy cupboard or on the nursery floor, and no one thought very much about him.

The Rabbit turns his thread-whiskered face toward the Skin Horse.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you.

(SOUND: soft hush of the fire)

SKIN HORSE
When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time.

The Skin Horse's worn button eye catches the firelight.

(SOUND: a log shifts; embers whisper)

SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby.

SKIN HORSE
But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse only smiles.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit sighs, longing and a little afraid of shabbiness.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

NANA — a brisk, aproned nurse with pinned hair — swoops about tidying. THE BOY stands in his nightshirt, searching.

(SOUND: cupboard doors thumping, Nana's hurried footsteps)

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She drags the Velveteen Rabbit out by one ear and puts him into the Boy's arms.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Moonlight through curtains. The Boy hugs the Rabbit very tight under the bedclothes, rolls over him, pushes him under the pillow. A night-light burns on the mantelpiece.

(SOUND: soft bedsprings, the Boy's whispered breathing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night, and for many nights after, the Velveteen Rabbit slept in the Boy's bed.

The Boy makes tunnels under the sheets.

THE BOY
(whispering)
Burrows. Like the real rabbits live in.

They play splendid games in whispers. When the Boy sleeps, the Rabbit snuggles under his warm chin, the Boy's hands clasped round him all night long.

(MUSIC: gentle lullaby theme, soft and small)

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Time passes in soft dissolves: the Rabbit's velveteen grows shabbier; his tail comes unsewn; pink rubs off his nose where the Boy has kissed him. He never notices.

(SOUND: night wind at the window)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And so time went on, and the little Rabbit was very happy — so happy that he never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Spring. Blossoms and raspberry canes. The Boy wheels the Rabbit in a barrow, builds fairy huts under the canes, digs burrows in the flower bed. The Rabbit comes up wet with dew and earthy.

(SOUND: birdsong, wheelbarrow squeak, bees in the border)

NANA
(finding him at dusk with a candle)
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The Boy sits up in bed and stretches out his hands. Nana holds the damp, earthy Rabbit.

(SOUND: night-light hiss, distant owl)

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Rabbit's boot-button eyes catch a look of wisdom and beauty.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the little Rabbit heard that he was happy, for he knew that what the Skin Horse had said was true at last. He was Real. The Boy himself had said it.

NANA
(next morning, lifting him)
I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

EXT. WOOD - EVENING

Long June evening. Bracken and tall trees near the house. The Boy settles the Rabbit in a cosy nest among the ferns, then wanders off to play.

(SOUND: leaves rustling, ants in dry grass, distant Boy's laughter)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That was a wonderful Summer!

Two WILD RABBITS creep from the tall bracken — furry, brand-new, seams invisible, shape changing as they move. They twitch their noses close to the Velveteen Rabbit.

(SOUND: soft padded feet on earth)

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT
Ho! It's as easy as anything.

The wild rabbit hops sideways and stands on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT
I don't believe you can!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Velveteen Rabbit freezes. His back is made all in one piece, like a pincushion.

(SOUND: bracken whispering)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to!

The wild rabbit stretches his neck and looks.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

WILD RABBIT
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

The wild rabbit whirls and dances. The Velveteen Rabbit grows dizzy with longing — a funny new tickly feeling runs through him.

(SOUND: feet thumping turf, then sudden stillness)

The strange rabbit comes so close his long whiskers brush the Velveteen Rabbit's ear — then wrinkles his nose, flattens his ears, jumps backwards.

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps. The Boy runs past. With a stamp and a flash of white tails the wild rabbits vanish.

(SOUND: stamp of feet, bracken swaying empty)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Only ants run to and fro. Little white moths flutter out as the sun sinks. The Boy returns and carries him home.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Velveteen Rabbit was all alone.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Weeks later. The Rabbit is very old and shabby — whiskers loved off, ear linings grey, brown spots faded, shape nearly gone. Still the Boy holds him close.

(SOUND: labored breathing, restless sheets)

The Boy's face is flushed. His little body burns so hot it scorches the Rabbit. Strange people come and go. A light burns all night. The Rabbit lies hidden under the bedclothes, never stirring.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And then, one day, the Boy was ill. Through it all the little Velveteen Rabbit lay there, afraid that if they found him some one might take him away.

He creeps to the pillow and whispers plans of gardens and raspberry thickets. The fever turns. The Boy sits up with picture-books; the Rabbit cuddles at his side.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - DAY

Bright sunny morning. Windows wide open. The Boy has been carried to the balcony in a shawl. The Rabbit peeps from the bedclothes. NANA and THE DOCTOR — a serious man with a black bag — stand in the room.

(SOUND: light curtains in the breeze, distant garden birds)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Boy was going to the seaside to-morrow. The room was to be disinfected, and all the books and toys that the Boy had played with in bed must be burnt.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

THE DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! — Burn it at once. Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

The Rabbit's head peeps from the sheets, listening, still hoping for the seaside.

EXT. GARDEN BY THE FOWL-HOUSE - EVENING

The end of the garden behind the fowl-house. A sack of old picture-books and rubbish. The gardener has been too busy; the bonfire waits for morning. The sack is untied. The Velveteen Rabbit wriggles his head through the opening.

(SOUND: evening insects, a hen murmuring, wind in raspberry canes)

He shivers. His coat is threadbare. He sees the raspberry thicket where they once played.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He thought of those long sunlit hours in the garden — how happy they were — and a great sadness came over him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A real tear trickles down his shabby velvet nose and falls to the ground.

(SOUND: a single soft drop on earth)

Where the tear falls, a mysterious flower pushes up — slender emerald leaves, a blossom like a golden cup. The blossom opens. Out steps the NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY — dress of pearl and dew-drops, flowers at her neck and in her hair, face like the most perfect flower.

(MUSIC: delicate chime, rising wonder)

She gathers him up and kisses his damp velveteen nose.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
(looking up)
...

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

Moon risen. Bracken fronds shine like frosted silver. She flies with him into the open glade. Wild rabbits dance with their shadows on the velvet grass — then stop and stand in a ring, staring.

(SOUND: soft night wind, many small feet on grass)

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses him again and sets him on the grass.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

He sits quite still, shy of his old one-piece back — and does not know the last kiss has changed him. Something tickles his nose. Without thinking he lifts a hind toe to scratch it.

(SOUND: a tiny scratch, then a gasp of joy)

He has hind legs. Brown fur, soft and shiny, instead of dingy velveteen. Ears twitch by themselves. Whiskers brush the grass.

He leaps. He springs about the turf, jumping sideways, whirling as the others do, wild with joy. When he stops to look for the Fairy, she is gone.

(MUSIC: bright leaping theme, full of freedom)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD - DAY

Spring again. Warm sunny days. THE BOY — a little taller — plays in the wood behind the house. Two rabbits creep from the bracken and peep. One is brown all over. The other has strange markings under his fur, as though long ago he had been spotted; about his soft nose and round black eyes something familiar.

(SOUND: birdsong, bracken parting, the Boy's quiet breath)

THE BOY
(to himself)
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The spotted rabbit watches the child who first helped him to be Real. Then both rabbits slip back into the green.

(MUSIC: gentle closing theme, tender and resolved)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

FADE OUT.

THE END
